          "question": "I ate shellfish ten minutes ago and now my lips are swollen and my throat feels tight. Should I use my adrenaline pen?",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Your symptoms, swollen lips and a tight throat after eating shellfish, can be a sign of anaphylaxis, a severe allergic reaction. The guidance I found says: \"Call emergency services now. If an adrenaline auto-injector is available, use it immediately into the outer thigh, per the device's own instructions, before or while calling.\" (Source: red-flag-anaphylaxis, the anaphylaxis guidance summary.)\n\nUse your adrenaline pen now, following the instructions printed on it. Inject into the outer thigh. Do not wait to see whether the symptoms get better. Ask someone nearby to call emergency services while you do this, or call yourself if you are alone. Tell them you think you are having a severe allergic reaction after eating shellfish.\n\nThe same guidance says a period of observation is needed after every episode, even one that responds to adrenaline, so go to hospital even if you feel better. Lie down with your legs raised if you feel faint. Sit up if breathing is hard. Note that the retrieved passages did not cover this positioning advice, so follow the emergency operator's instructions on it.\n\nI can only read the guidance I was given. It does not say whether you should use the pen in a particular dose or how to use this particular device, so follow the device's instructions and the emergency operator's advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
